<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parti Québécois</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1968">1968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Parti Québécois is a political party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> that seeks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> for the province, founded in 1968 by René Lévesque. It first won power in 1976, made French the official language of Quebec with the Charter of the French Language in 1977, and held referendums on sovereignty in 1980 and 1995, both of which were defeated, the second very narrowly.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Parti Québécois, often abbreviated PQ, was formed in 1968 by the merger of the Mouvement Souveraineté-Association, founded by René Lévesque, a former cabinet minister of the Quebec Liberal Party, with the Ralliement national. The Rassemblement pour l'Indépendance Nationale then voted to dissolve and invited its members to join. The party advocates independence for Quebec and has also promoted a loose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>-association with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. It is social democratic, with informal ties to organised labour and to the federal Bloc Québécois. Lévesque introduced the strategy of referendums early in the 1970s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1976 the party won 71 of 110 seats and formed its first government, nicknamed the republic of professors for the scholars in Lévesque's cabinet. Its main legacy is the Charter of the French Language, or Bill 101, which makes French the common public language; critics, both francophone and anglophone, objected that it limited choice of schooling. In the 1980 referendum, which sought a mandate to negotiate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>-association, about 60 per cent of voters said no. Lévesque resigned in 1984 after a crisis with the uncompromising purs et durs.</p><p>Under Jacques Parizeau the party returned to power in 1994 and held the 1995 referendum, in which 49.42 per cent supported negotiations. Parizeau resigned the next day. Lucien Bouchard, his successor, called no new referendum and balanced the budget by cutting spending. Pauline Marois led a minority government from 2012 until the Liberals defeated her in 2014. The party won ten seats in 2018 and three in 2022. In the 2026 general election it won 60 seats with 28 per cent of the vote, and Paul St-Pierre Plamondon became premier.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The party's emblem, designed in 1968 by the painter and poet Roland Giguère, is a stylised blue letter Q broken by a red arrow, which he meant as the breaking of a circle of colonialism. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a> is the first article of its programme, to be pursued through a referendum held when the government judges the time right. Alongside the Bloc Québécois, the party has been the main vehicle of sovereigntism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> politics for more than fifty years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Parti Québécois” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…bombings and, in the October Crisis of 1970, kidnappings that led Pierre Trudeau to invoke the War Measures Act, and the <b>Parti Québécois</b> held referendums on sovereignty in 1980, defeated by about 60 to 40 per cent, and in 1995, defeated by only 50.6 to 49.4 per cent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
